We continue to consider the tensor product <<ref:example fock>>.
The matrix $\hat B$ is given by

\begin{align}
&\hat B=
\begin{pmatrix}
t_2^2       &t_2t_3 C\mu^{-1}         &     0     &     0     &       0       & t_1t_2      \\
t_2t_3C^{-1}\mu & -t_2t_3(s_1+s_1^{-1}) & t_2t_3  &    0      &         0     & 0     \\
0  &t_2t_3               &-t_2t_3(s_1+s_1^{-1})& t_2t_3   &  0 &    0           \\
0  &      0   &t_2t_3                &t_3^2  & t_1t_3       &      0         \\
0 &     0    &     0                 &t_1t_3  &-t_1t_3(s_2+s_2^{-1})&t_1t_3     \\
t_1t_2      &    0    &    0        &     0     &t_1t_3                    &t_1^2    \\
\end{pmatrix}.
\end{align}

With the root $A_0(z)$ the Dynkin diagram becomes the following.

\begin{align}
\raisebox{-20pt}{\begin{tikzpicture}[baseline=(origin.base)]
\dynkin[root radius=.1cm, edge length=1.3cm, labels={{2},1,1,3,2,1},extended, affine mark=t]{A}{ootot}
\end{tikzpicture}}
\end{align}

The affine node is fermionic and we label it by 2, since it corresponds to the ratio of the last and first terms obtained from $\F_3(u_6)$ and $\F_1(u_1)$.
However, note that there is an arbitrary parameter $\mu$ which does not appear in the diagram. \qed
